{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Jacob saw, etc. And HaShem said to Jacob.</b> One needs to know what connection and relationship there is between these two verses." "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is to preface two things because of which Jacob did what he did — that he fled without Laban's knowledge — for without this he would not have left without Laban's knowledge, or he would still have tarried there many days. That is, had he not seen Laban's countenance and the words they spoke, even though the prophecy came to him to depart, he would have gone with Laban's knowledge, for Jacob was not sold to him as a slave, and he was with him of his own will. But on account of the words of his sons and Laban's changed countenance, he feared lest he would strip him of all his labor — and this is what he said in the end (Genesis 31:31): ״כִּי יָרֵאתִי פֶּן תִּגְזֹל״, etc. And had the prophecy not come to him to depart, even though he heard the words of Laban's sons, he would not have rushed to leave at that time, but would have waited until he could cunningly secure his desire over the course of many days and then separate from him, and would have stayed there [more] days. Thus it was through the convergence of both things — the words of Laban's sons and the change in his countenance, together with the word of HaShem — that he did all that is recounted in the matter. Therefore the verse sets them side by side."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:10.652796327Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}